  "protocol": {
    "ab_split_rule": "half = 'B' if int(sha256(session_id)[:2], 16) % 2 else 'A'",
    "floor": {
      "and_the_honest_half": "meetability by construction is exactly what makes the STRANGER non-claim binding. These sessions can reach the floor because their author knows what a binding-dependent turn is. Nothing here says a stranger's sessions would, and no stranger-usability claim is made — DESIGN-plain-input §5 G1's park is cited, not re-encountered.",
      "floor_verbatim": "Floor: ≥**30** sessions, ≥**120** turns, ≥**36** binding-dependent turns in half B's share alone.",
      "if_unreachable": "STOP. Publish 'multi-turn binding is rare in practice' as the cycle's finding and build nothing further. A published stop is a result; a quiet descope is not.",
      "meetability_argument": "By construction, and the construction is the whole argument: the sessions are maintainer-authored, so their count, length and assumption structure are chosen before recording rather than sampled from a population that might not contain them. 60 sessions at the cap clears 30. Sixty sessions averaging six turns clears 120 with a wide margin and stays far under the 64-turn-per-session cap. For the 36: a binding-dependent turn is one whose served evaluation READ a live assumption's normal_form, so authoring a session with a `suppose x = 5` declaration followed by three turns that compute with x produces three binding-dependent turns deterministically. Authoring roughly forty assumption-bearing sessions with three or more such turns each puts well over a hundred binding-dependent turns in the corpus; a hash-derived split sends approximately half to B, so B's share is expected near sixty against a floor of thirty-six. The margin is deliberate: the floor must survive an unlucky split, not an average one.",
      "reading_frozen_here": "30 sessions and 120 turns over the RECORDED CORPUS; 36 binding-dependent turns in HALF B's share alone.",
      "why_this_reading_and_the_discrepancy_it_resolves": "ROADMAP-v0.21 §1.1 compresses the same floor to 'Floor in half B's share alone: ≥30 sessions, ≥120 turns, ≥36 binding-dependent turns', attaching the half-B qualifier to all three. The design's own sentence attaches it to the last clause, and the design is the governing document (§7-§9 all scope the GATE to half B while §6 P3 describes the CORPUS). Recorded here rather than left to be discovered: under the roadmap's reading, 30 sessions in half B against a 60-session cap would be a coin flip on a hash-derived split — an unmeetable-by-construction floor of exactly the shape §4.0(3) was written after C-E1 to forbid. The design's reading is both the grammatical one and the meetable one, and the choice is stated instead of made silently."
    },
    "live_assumption_cap": 8,
    "recorder_code_digest": "e3070dd3b118aee7681b8091b5db6a434ba6c54551081794228e9eb91d087c43",
    "session_count_cap": 60,
    "session_id_rule": "session ids are assigned before recording by the frozen rule `v021-s{NN}` for NN in 01..60, in order. The ids are therefore fixed by this file, not chosen after seeing which half they land in — the split is a consequence of the ids, and the ids are a consequence of a counter.",
    "turn_cap_per_session": 64
  },
